Title: dvc gc from a worktree can delete blobs the primary checkout still needs
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T18:32Z HDMX-coding-agent created
2026-07-27T18:36Z HDMX-coding-agent note raised by the review-pr panel on PR #1179 and reproduced there; filed rather than fixed in that PR because a guard is a different change from the hook fix

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0360 pointed every worktree's `.dvc/cache` at the primary checkout's, so
`make data` can work in a worktree. That is the right fix, and it widens the
blast radius of exactly one command.

`dvc gc` prunes by reachability computed from **whichever checkout invokes
it**. Before 0360 a worktree's cache was private and empty, so running it there
was near-harmless. Now a single 19 GB cache sits behind every checkout, and a
worktree pinned to an old `dvc.lock`, or one mid-rebuild that has not pushed,
computes a smaller reachable set than the primary's and deletes the difference.
The review panel on PR #1179 reproduced this, sequentially, with no race
involved.

The repo has already paid for this lesson once in a narrower form:
`tickets/closed/0252-*.erg` names "the dvc gc caveat" as the reason 8 orphan
pointers were deliberately kept.

## Why prose is not enough

0360 documented the hazard in `.claude/rules/architecture.md` § Data location
and in the hook comment. That is the right place for the explanation, but a
comment does not stop a data-destroying command, and the loss is silent until
someone needs a blob that is gone. The remote (`padme`) makes it recoverable,
which lowers the severity — it does not make it a non-event.

## Relevant files

- `.githooks/post-checkout` — creates the shared-cache symlink
- `.claude/rules/architecture.md` § Data location — where the hazard is written down
- `Makefile` — where a wrapper target would live
- `tickets/closed/0252-*.erg` — the earlier encounter with the caveat

## Actions

1. Decide the mechanism. Options, cheapest first:
   - a `gc:` Make target that refuses to run when the invoking checkout is a
     worktree (`git rev-parse --git-common-dir` differs from `.git`), telling
     the caller to run it from the primary checkout;
   - the same check as a guard script, so it also catches a bare `dvc gc`;
   - `--workspace` scoping conventions documented and enforced in the target.
2. Implement the chosen guard.
3. Point the architecture.md note at it, so the prose describes a mechanism
   rather than asking for care.

## Known limit of the Makefile-refusal design

`.dvc/tmp/lock` is per-worktree, so it does not serialize across checkouts. A
`make gc` refusal catches the sequential wrong-cwd case, which is the one
actually observed, but not a live `dvc gc` racing a sibling worktree's reader.
Raised by the review panel on PR #1179. If the cheap guard is chosen, say so
here rather than implying full coverage.

## Test

Integration, and it must assert the refusal rather than the wording:

    # from a worktree
    make gc            # exits non-zero, cache untouched, message names the primary
    # from the primary checkout
    make gc            # proceeds

Assert the cache is unchanged after the refused run (entry count, or a sentinel
blob that must survive).

## Verification

- [ ] `dvc gc` (or the wrapping target) refuses from a worktree
- [ ] It still works from the primary checkout
- [ ] A refused invocation leaves the cache byte-identical

## Invariants

- The 0360 sharing stays — this ticket guards the hazard, it does not undo the
  fix by giving worktrees private caches again.
- No guard may make an ordinary `make data` slower or interactive.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A worktree cannot prune the shared cache without an explicit, deliberate
      override, and the architecture.md note points at the mechanism instead of
      asking the reader to remember.
